    \item $\mathrm{II} < 0$: \textbf{sub-additive}---the compound effect is less than the sum of parts.
    \item $\mathrm{II} = 0$: \textbf{additive}---the two mechanisms do not interact.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Choice of additive null.}
Equation~\ref{eq:ii} posits an additive null $r_b = r_a + r_k - 1$ rather than multiplicative independence $r_b = r_a \cdot r_k$.
We adopt the additive form because head pruning and KV cache eviction operate on disjoint model dimensions---structural (which heads to retain) versus positional (which tokens to cache)---so their degradations sum rather than multiply at the sparsity levels we study ($s \leq 0.30$).
We verify in Appendix~\ref{app:multiplicative-null} that qualitative conclusions are unchanged under the multiplicative null.

Normalizing by $\text{PPL}_d$ ensures cross-problem comparability.
Because the II distribution is heavily right-skewed, we report medians as the central tendency.

\paragraph{Severity gradient.}
To capture how II varies across reasoning phases (defined in \S\ref{sec:phase-segmentation}), we define the \emph{severity gradient}:
\begin{equation}

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

We present our empirical findings in five parts: strategy-dependent compound severity (\S\ref{sec:strategy-severity}), phase-modulated amplification (\S\ref{sec:phase-amplification}), the phase-strategy crossover (\S\ref{sec:crossover}), cross-model replication (\S\ref{sec:cross-model}), and statistical validation (\S\ref{sec:stat-validation}).


\subsection{Experimental Overview}
\label{sec:exp-overview}

We evaluate compound sparsity on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B using a 50-problem stratified subset of MATH-500 (\S\ref{sec:dataset}). For each problem, we measure perplexity under four conditions---dense, attention-only pruning, KV-only eviction, and compound (both)---at sparsity levels $s \in \{0.15, 0.20, 0.25, 0.30\}$. We compare importance-based and random eviction strategies (\S\ref{sec:sparsity-config}), and segment each generated sequence into high-, mid-, and low-sparsity reasoning phases via quantile-based attention sparsity partitioning (\S\ref{sec:phase-seg}). The interaction index (II, Eq.~\ref{eq:ii}) quantifies compound super-additivity, and the severity gradient ($G$, Eq.~\ref{eq:gradient}) captures phase-dependent amplification.
Models are evaluated in \texttt{bfloat16} precision (\S\ref{sec:experimental-setup}). Full experimental details appear therein.


\subsection{Strategy-Dependent Severity (C1)}
\label{sec:strategy-severity}

The compound severity difference reveals a stark dependence on eviction strategy. Table~\ref{tab:aggregate-ii} reports the median compound severity difference $\Delta = \widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\textit{low}} - \widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\textit{high}}$ across 50 problems for each strategy and sparsity level. Under importance-based eviction, compound sparsity produces substantial super-additive degradation that grows with sparsity: $\Delta$ increases from $0.39$ at $s{=}0.15$ to $14.59$ at $s{=}0.30$, a $37{\times}$ amplification. Under random eviction, the severity difference shows no systematic growth, remaining near zero ($-0.02$ to $1.54$). % exp_id: bf16_importance_s015..s030, bf16_ds_rand_s015..s030; source: experiment_summary_table.json

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Median compound severity difference ($\Delta = \widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\textit{low}} - \widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\textit{high}}$) by eviction strategy and sparsity level on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B ($n{=}50$ MATH-500 problems).}
\label{tab:aggregate-ii}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}
\toprule
Strategy & $s{=}0.15$ & $s{=}0.20$ & $s{=}0.25$ & $s{=}0.30$ \\
\midrule
Importance & 0.39 & 1.48 & 7.16 & 14.59 \\ % exp_id: bf16_importance_s015..s030; source: experiment_summary_table.json median_diff
Random     & 0.01 & $-$0.02 & 0.54 & 1.54 \\ % exp_id: bf16_ds_rand_s015..s030; source: experiment_summary_table.json median_diff
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

This strategy dependence indicates that compound sparsity severity is not an intrinsic property of the compression level. Rather, the same sparsity configuration produces qualitatively different compound behavior depending on the eviction strategy---an emergent phase-strategy interaction whose structure we unpack in the following subsections.


\subsection{Phase-Modulated Amplification (C2)}
\label{sec:phase-amplification}

Decomposing the interaction index by reasoning phase reveals that the strategy-dependent severity observed in \S\ref{sec:strategy-severity} is driven by amplification concentrated in the low-sparsity phase. Table~\ref{tab:main-results} reports per-phase median II and the severity gradient $G$ at each sparsity level.

Under importance-based eviction, the severity gradient---the ratio of low-phase to high-phase median II---increases with sparsity from $69{\times}$ at $s{=}0.15$ to $274{\times}$ at $s{=}0.30$. The low-sparsity phase---corresponding to exploratory reasoning with broadly distributed attention---bears disproportionate compound degradation, while the high-sparsity phase---corresponding to structured computation with focused attention---experiences near-zero compound interaction ($\widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\textit{high}}$ ranges from $0.006$ to $0.053$). Under random eviction, the gradient remains near unity ($G \approx 0.9$--$1.7{\times}$) at all sparsity levels, confirming that phase modulation is specific to the emergent interaction between head pruning and importance-based scoring. % exp_id: bf16_importance_s015..s030, bf16_ds_rand_s015..s030; source: experiment_summary_table.json

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Interaction index (II) by eviction strategy, reasoning phase, and sparsity level. II values are problem-level medians across 50 problems. $\Delta = \widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\textit{low}} - \widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\textit{high}}$ is the median severity difference. $G = \widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\textit{low}} / \widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\textit{high}}$ quantifies phase-dependent amplification.}
\label{tab:main-results}
\begin{tabular}{@{}llccccr@{}}
\toprule
$s$ & Strategy & $\widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\textit{high}}$ & $\widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\textit{mid}}$ & $\widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\textit{low}}$ & $\Delta$ & $G$ \\
\midrule
0.15 & Importance & 0.006 & 0.081 & 0.398 & 0.39 & $\mathbf{69}$ \\ % exp_id: bf16_importance_s015
0.15 & Random     & 0.093 & 0.097 & 0.103 & 0.01 & 1.1 \\ % exp_id: bf16_ds_rand_s015
\addlinespace
0.20 & Importance & 0.015 & 0.203 & 1.496 & 1.48 & $\mathbf{103}$ \\ % exp_id: bf16_importance_s020
0.20 & Random     & 0.292 & 0.350 & 0.276 & $-$0.02 & 0.9 \\ % exp_id: bf16_ds_rand_s020
\addlinespace
0.25 & Importance & 0.032 & 1.118 & 7.196 & 7.16 & $\mathbf{222}$ \\ % exp_id: bf16_importance_s025
0.25 & Random     & 0.818 & 0.932 & 1.363 & 0.54 & 1.7 \\ % exp_id: bf16_ds_rand_s025
\addlinespace
0.30 & Importance & 0.053 & 2.283 & 14.639 & 14.59 & $\mathbf{274}$ \\ % exp_id: bf16_importance_s030
0.30 & Random     & 2.503 & 2.592 & 4.041 & 1.54 & 1.6 \\ % exp_id: bf16_ds_rand_s030
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The gradient growth is sub-exponential: successive ratios of the median severity difference $\Delta$ between adjacent sparsity levels are $3.8{\times}$, $4.8{\times}$, and $2.0{\times}$---a decreasing terminal growth rate consistent with a ceiling effect as compound degradation in the low-sparsity phase approaches saturation. The severity gradient $G$ exhibits the same pattern, with consecutive ratios of $1.5{\times}$, $2.2{\times}$, and $1.2{\times}$. Figure~\ref{fig:hero} visualizes this trajectory, contrasting the steep importance-based curve against the flat random baseline. % exp_id: bf16_importance_s015..s030; source: experiment_summary_table.json median_diff

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig1_dose_response.pdf}
\caption{Severity gradient ($G$) as a function of sparsity level $s$. Importance-based eviction (solid) exhibits an increasing gradient from $69{\times}$ to $274{\times}$, while random eviction (dashed) remains flat near $1{\times}$. Shaded regions denote 95\% bootstrap confidence intervals.} % exp_id: bf16_importance_s015..s030, bf16_ds_rand_s015..s030
\label{fig:hero}
\end{figure}

We report medians throughout because the II distribution is right-skewed: the mean severity difference exceeds the median by a factor of $1.6{:}1$ at $s{=}0.30$ under importance-based eviction (mean $\Delta = 22.94$ vs.\ median $\Delta = 14.59$). The median resists the influence of outlier problems with exceptionally long reasoning traces, while bootstrap confidence intervals (\S\ref{sec:stat-validation}) account for this skewness via the resampling distribution. % exp_id: bf16_importance_s030; source: cohens_d_bf16.json mean_diff=22.939, experiment_summary_table.json median_diff=14.586


\subsection{Phase-Strategy Crossover (C3)}
\label{sec:crossover}

The phase-modulated gradient produces a striking qualitative phenomenon: a \emph{crossover} in which importance-based eviction is simultaneously the better and worse strategy depending on the reasoning phase. At $s{=}0.25$, importance-based eviction yields a lower II than random in the high-sparsity phase ($\widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\textit{high}} = 0.032$ vs.\ $0.818$), but a higher II in the low-sparsity phase ($\widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\textit{low}} = 7.196$ vs.\ $1.363$). This is not merely a magnitude difference---it is a reversal in the relative ranking of strategies across phases. % exp_id: bf16_importance_s025, bf16_ds_rand_s025; source: experiment_summary_table.json

Figure~\ref{fig:crossover} displays the crossover structure as a heatmap of per-phase II values across strategies and sparsity levels.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig3_crossover.pdf}
\caption{Strategy-dependent crossover at $s{=}0.25$. Grouped bar chart of median II by eviction strategy and reasoning phase. Importance-based eviction outperforms random in the high-sparsity phase but produces $5.3{\times}$ higher II in the low-sparsity phase, reversing the strategy ranking.} % exp_id: bf16_importance_s025, bf16_ds_rand_s025; 7.196/1.363=5.28
\label{fig:crossover}
\end{figure}

The crossover emerges from an emergent phase-strategy interaction: in the high-sparsity phase---where attention is already focused---importance-based eviction preserves genuinely critical positions, yielding lower compound severity than random eviction. In the low-sparsity phase, however, head pruning redistributes attention mass across surviving heads, altering the importance score landscape in a way that couples the two sparsity mechanisms (\S\ref{sec:analysis}). The result is that targeted eviction inadvertently removes positions that would have been retained under the pre-pruning importance ranking---producing \emph{higher} compound severity than the explicitly random baseline.

The crossover is present at all four tested sparsity levels ($s \geq 0.15$): at each level, importance-based eviction achieves lower II in the high-sparsity phase but higher II in the low-sparsity phase relative to random eviction. The magnitude of the low-phase reversal grows from $3.9{\times}$ at $s{=}0.15$ to $5.4{\times}$ at $s{=}0.20$, before moderating to $3.6{\times}$ at $s{=}0.30$ as random eviction severity also rises. % exp_id: bf16_importance_s015..s030, bf16_ds_rand_s015..s030; source: experiment_summary_table.json; ratios: 0.398/0.103=3.87, 1.496/0.276=5.42, 7.196/1.363=5.28, 14.639/4.041=3.62


\subsection{Cross-Model Replication (C5)}
\label{sec:cross-model}

To assess whether the phase-modulated severity gradient generalizes beyond DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B, we replicate the importance-based eviction experiments on Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct. Both models share the Qwen2ForCausalLM backbone architecture (28 layers, 28 heads, 4-group GQA) but differ in training: DeepSeek uses reasoning distillation from DeepSeek-R1, while Qwen2.5 uses standard instruction tuning without explicit reasoning delimiters. This controlled comparison isolates the effect of training methodology on the compound sparsity interaction.

Despite lacking explicit \texttt{<think>} delimiters, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct exhibits sufficient variation in attention sparsity across its generation trajectory to permit phase segmentation via the same quantile-based method (\S\ref{sec:phase-seg}). Four of the 50 problems are excluded due to degenerate phase assignments, yielding $n{=}46$ valid problems for Qwen (vs.\ $n{=}50$ for DeepSeek).

Table~\ref{tab:cross-model} compares the median severity difference across both models.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-model replication: median compound severity difference ($\Delta = \widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\textit{low}} - \widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\textit{high}}$) under importance-based eviction. Both models share the Qwen2 architecture but differ in training methodology (distillation vs.\ instruction tuning).}
\label{tab:cross-model}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}
\toprule
Model & $s{=}0.15$ & $s{=}0.20$ & $s{=}0.25$ & $s{=}0.30$ \\
\midrule
DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B & 0.39 & 1.48 & 7.16 & 14.59 \\ % exp_id: bf16_importance_s015..s030; source: experiment_summary_table.json
Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct         & 0.04 & 0.24 & 1.27 & 2.94 \\ % exp_id: bf16_qw_imp_s015..s030; source: experiment_summary_table.json; 0.235 rounds to 0.24 at 2dp
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct qualitatively reproduces the monotonic severity gradient observed in DeepSeek. The median severity difference increases from $0.04$ at $s{=}0.15$ to $2.94$ at $s{=}0.30$ (Table~\ref{tab:cross-model}), paralleling DeepSeek's $0.39$-to-$14.59$ trajectory at approximately one-tenth to one-fifth the absolute magnitude. Both models yield strictly increasing paired $d_z$ trajectories (DeepSeek: $0.44 \to 0.80$; Qwen: $0.28 \to 1.02$; Table~\ref{tab:cohens-d}). Figure~\ref{fig:cross-model} visualizes the dual-model severity trajectory. Spearman $\rho = 1.0$ between the four-point $d_z$ sequences (gradient permutation $p = 0.043$). The per-problem concordance across models is not significant (mean $\rho = -0.11$, $p = 0.926$), indicating that while the aggregate gradient direction replicates, the specific problems driving severity differ between models---consistent with model-specific attention patterns rather than a universal per-problem vulnerability ranking. The Qwen effect is marginal at $s{=}0.15$ ($d_z{=}0.28$, bootstrap CI includes zero), becoming reliable at $s \geq 0.20$ ($d_z \geq 0.55$); this onset lag relative to DeepSeek is consistent with the lower overall compound severity in instruction-tuned models lacking explicit reasoning structure. % exp_id: cross_model_analysis.json (spearman_rho=1.0, gradient_permutation_p=0.043, per_problem_concordance_p=0.926, mean_rho=-0.107); cohens_d_bf16_qwen.json

As a control, random eviction on Qwen yields no systematic phase gradient at three of four sparsity levels (3/4 FAIL), mirroring DeepSeek's 4/4 FAIL pattern. At $s{=}0.30$, Qwen random produces a borderline PASS (permutation $p = 0.012$) but with negligible effect size (Cohen's $d = -0.04$, CI spans zero), indicating no meaningful phase modulation. % exp_id: bf16_qw_rand_s015..s030; source: experiment_summary_table.json

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig5_crossmodel.pdf}
\caption{Cross-model comparison of the median severity difference under importance-based eviction. Both DeepSeek (solid) and Qwen (dashed) exhibit monotonically increasing $\Delta$ with sparsity; Qwen's magnitude is approximately one-fifth of DeepSeek's.} % exp_id: bf16_importance_s015..s030, bf16_qw_imp_s015..s030
\label{fig:cross-model}
\end{figure}

The replication on a second model with distinct training methodology suggests that the phase-modulated severity gradient is not an artifact of DeepSeek's distillation-induced phase structure, but rather an emergent property of how compound sparsity interacts with attention dynamics in Transformer-based reasoning. We note that both models share the same backbone architecture; generalization to other architectures (e.g., LLaMA, Mistral) remains an open question (\S\ref{sec:limitations}).


\subsection{Statistical Validation}
\label{sec:stat-validation}

We validate the strategy-dependent severity using two complementary approaches: a paired permutation test on the difference statistic for significance, and paired $d_z$ for effect size.

\paragraph{Permutation test.}
For each problem $i$ and sparsity level $s$, we compute the compound severity difference $\Delta_i^{(\text{strat})} = \mathrm{II}_{\textit{low},i} - \mathrm{II}_{\textit{high},i}$ under each eviction strategy. The test statistic is the median of the paired difference $\widetilde{\Delta} = \text{median}_i\!\bigl(\Delta_i^{(\text{imp})} - \Delta_i^{(\text{rand})}\bigr)$, measuring whether importance-based eviction produces systematically larger phase-dependent severity than random eviction. We permute strategy labels $10{,}000$ times to construct the null distribution and apply Bonferroni correction for 4 sparsity-level comparisons.

Table~\ref{tab:permutation} reports the results. All four sparsity levels reject $H_0$ (no strategy difference) after Bonferroni correction ($p_{\text{Bonf}} < 0.005$), with observed median differences increasing monotonically from $0.37$ at $s{=}0.15$ to $9.85$ at $s{=}0.30$. % exp_id: deepseek_bf16_permtest; source: permutation_test_results.json

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Paired permutation test (difference statistic) for compound severity: importance vs.\ random eviction on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B ($n{=}49$ paired problems, $10{,}000$ permutations). $p$-values are Bonferroni-corrected for 4 comparisons.}
\label{tab:permutation}
\begin{tabular}{@{}rrcl@{}}
\toprule
$s$ & Observed $\widetilde{\Delta}$ & 95\% CI & $p_{\text{Bonf}}$ \\
\midrule
0.15 & 0.37 & $[0.19,\; 0.62]$ & 0.0032 \\ % exp_id: deepseek_bf16_permtest_s015; source: permutation_test_results.json s015.permutation_test_difference
0.20 & 0.97 & $[0.69,\; 1.49]$ & ${<}\,0.001$ \\ % exp_id: deepseek_bf16_permtest_s020; source: permutation_test_results.json s020.permutation_test_difference
0.25 & 3.34 & $[1.66,\; 6.64]$ & ${<}\,0.001$ \\ % exp_id: deepseek_bf16_permtest_s025; source: permutation_test_results.json s025.permutation_test_difference
0.30 & 9.85 & $[6.70,\; 16.29]$ & ${<}\,0.001$ \\ % exp_id: deepseek_bf16_permtest_s030; source: permutation_test_results.json s030.permutation_test_difference
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The monotonic growth of both the point estimate and the CI lower bound ($0.19 \to 0.69 \to 1.66 \to 6.70$) confirms that compound severity amplification under importance-based eviction strengthens with sparsity---not merely in central tendency but also in worst-case lower bounds. Figure~\ref{fig:permtest-forest} presents these results as a forest plot. % exp_id: deepseek_bf16_permtest; source: permutation_test_results.json

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig4_bootstrap.pdf}
\caption{Forest plot of permutation test results for the difference statistic. Horizontal bars show 95\% permutation CIs; diamonds mark observed median differences $\widetilde{\Delta}$. All CIs lie entirely above zero (vertical dashed line), confirming significant strategy-dependent severity at every sparsity level.}
\label{fig:permtest-forest}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Effect sizes.}
Table~\ref{tab:cohens-d} reports paired $d_z$ (bootstrap $B{=}10{,}000$) measuring the standardized difference in II between low- and high-sparsity phases under importance-based eviction.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Paired $d_z$ and per-problem median difference $\widetilde{\Delta}$ for the phase-dependent II (low $-$ high) under importance-based eviction. Bootstrap 95\% CIs for $\widetilde{\Delta}$ ($B{=}10{,}000$, per-problem paired). DeepSeek: $n{=}49$ problems (1 excluded); Qwen: $n{=}46$ problems (4 excluded).}
\label{tab:cohens-d}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}
\toprule
$s$ & DeepSeek $d_z$ & $\widetilde{\Delta}$ [95\% CI] & Qwen $d_z$ & $\widetilde{\Delta}$ [95\% CI] \\
\midrule
0.15 & 0.44 (small) & 0.32\; $[0.20,\; 0.47]$ & 0.28 (small) & 0.05\; $[-0.01,\; 0.11]$\textsuperscript{ns} \\ % exp_id: bf16_ds_imp_s015, bf16_qw_imp_s015; per-problem paired B=10000; d_z from cohens_d_bf16.json + cohens_d_bf16_qwen.json
0.20 & 0.52 (medium) & 1.15\; $[0.66,\; 1.26]$ & 0.55 (medium) & 0.21\; $[0.07,\; 0.40]$ \\ % exp_id: bf16_ds_imp_s020, bf16_qw_imp_s020; per-problem paired B=10000; d_z from cohens_d_bf16.json + cohens_d_bf16_qwen.json
0.25 & 0.68 (medium) & 3.97\; $[2.83,\; 5.29]$ & 0.89 (large) & 1.11\; $[0.61,\; 1.47]$ \\ % exp_id: bf16_ds_imp_s025, bf16_qw_imp_s025; per-problem paired B=10000; d_z from cohens_d_bf16.json + cohens_d_bf16_qwen.json
0.30 & 0.80 (large) & 9.94\; $[8.39,\; 16.65]$ & 1.02 (large) & 2.62\; $[1.72,\; 3.65]$ \\ % exp_id: bf16_ds_imp_s030, bf16_qw_imp_s030; per-problem paired B=10000; d_z from cohens_d_bf16.json + cohens_d_bf16_qwen.json
\bottomrule
\multicolumn{5}{@{}l}{\footnotesize \textsuperscript{ns}CI spans zero; marginal at $s{=}0.15$ (bootstrap CI includes zero), becoming reliable at $s \geq 0.20$.}
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

For DeepSeek, the effect size increases monotonically from small ($d_z{=}0.44$) at $s{=}0.15$ to large ($d_z{=}0.80$) at $s{=}0.30$, with all bootstrap CIs for $\widetilde{\Delta}$ excluding zero (Table~\ref{tab:cohens-d}). % exp_id: bf16_ds_imp_s015..s030; per-problem paired B=10000
For Qwen, the compound interaction effect is marginal at $s{=}0.15$ ($d_z{=}0.28$, bootstrap CI includes zero), becoming reliable at $s \geq 0.20$ ($d_z \geq 0.55$), suggesting a model-dependent onset threshold for detectable phase modulation. % exp_id: bf16_qw_imp_s015..s030; per-problem paired B=10000
At higher sparsity levels ($s \geq 0.25$), Qwen exhibits larger standardized effect sizes than DeepSeek ($d_z{=}0.89$/$1.02$ vs.\ $0.68$/$0.80$), consistent with lower between-problem variance in Qwen's II distribution yielding larger standardized differences despite smaller absolute II values. % exp_id: bf16_ds_imp_s025..s030, bf16_qw_imp_s025..s030; per-problem paired B=10000
Full per-problem distributions and permutation null distributions appear in Appendix~\ref{app:bootstrap-details}.

\section{Analysis: Mechanisms of Phase-Modulated Severity}
\label{sec:analysis}

The results in \S\ref{sec:results} reveal a striking asymmetry: importance-based eviction exhibits $69$--$274{\times}$ severity amplification in low-sparsity phases (Table~\ref{tab:main-results}), while random eviction remains flat at ${\sim}1{\times}$ across all phases. % exp_id: bf16_importance_s015..s030; G=69,103,222,274
Why is compound severity phase-modulated for one strategy but not the other? We identify two mechanisms. \emph{Discrimination collapse}---a systematic reduction in the discriminative power of KV importance scores under head pruning---is the primary mechanism, robustly observed across both models and all tested conditions. A secondary \emph{entropy redistribution} pattern is consistent for DeepSeek but only partially replicates on Qwen, indicating model-dependent modulation.

\subsection{Discrimination Collapse Under Head Pruning}
\label{sec:discrimination-collapse}

The KV importance score (Eq.~\ref{eq:kv-importance}) aggregates attention mass across surviving heads. When head pruning reduces the number of contributing heads, the cumulative importance distribution becomes more uniform: the gap between highly-attended and rarely-attended positions shrinks. We quantify this effect through two complementary metrics applied to the per-position importance distribution.

The \textbf{Gini coefficient} measures inequality in the importance distribution ($\mathrm{Gini}{=}0$: perfectly uniform; $\mathrm{Gini}{=}1$: maximally concentrated). The \textbf{coefficient of variation} (CV) captures relative dispersion. A decrease in either metric after pruning indicates reduced discriminative power.

We observe discrimination collapse across both models in all tested conditions (Table~\ref{tab:discrimination-metrics}). Under importance-based eviction, the Gini delta is negative at every sparsity level for both DeepSeek ($-0.030$ to $-0.081$) and Qwen ($-0.010$ to $-0.020$), with magnitude scaling monotonically with sparsity in DeepSeek. % exp_id: c4_entropy_deepseek, c4_entropy_qwen
The CV delta is likewise negative in all 8 importance-based conditions, ranging from $-0.56$ to $-1.66$ (DeepSeek) and $-0.05$ to $-1.49$ (Qwen). The same pattern holds under random eviction (DeepSeek: $-0.031$ to $-0.073$; Qwen: $-0.019$ to $-0.045$), confirming that discrimination collapse is a consequence of head pruning \emph{per se}, not of the eviction strategy.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Discrimination collapse under importance-based eviction. $\Delta\mathrm{Gini}$ and $\Delta\mathrm{CV}$ are post-pruning minus baseline; negative values indicate reduced discriminative power. All 8 conditions show consistent negative deltas across both models.}
\label{tab:discrimination-metrics}
\small
\begin{tabular}{llrr}
\toprule
Model & $s$ & $\Delta\mathrm{Gini}$ & $\Delta\mathrm{CV}$ \\
\midrule
DeepSeek & $0.15$ & $-0.030$ & $-0.56$ \\
DeepSeek & $0.20$ & $-0.040$ & $-0.77$ \\
DeepSeek & $0.25$ & $-0.065$ & $-1.29$ \\
DeepSeek & $0.30$ & $-0.081$ & $-1.66$ \\
\midrule
Qwen & $0.15$ & $-0.010$ & $-0.05$ \\
Qwen & $0.20$ & $-0.012$ & $-0.21$ \\
Qwen & $0.25$ & $-0.010$ & $-1.49$ \\
Qwen & $0.30$ & $-0.020$ & $-1.14$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

This cross-model consistency establishes discrimination collapse as a robust consequence of compound sparsity. When importance scores lose discriminative power, thresholding the bottom-$s$ fraction becomes noise-sensitive: small perturbations change which positions cross the eviction boundary. In the limit of perfectly uniform scores, importance-based eviction degenerates into an arbitrary selection functionally equivalent to biased random eviction. The severity inversion observed in \S\ref{sec:crossover} is thus an emergent consequence of the interaction between head pruning and importance scoring conditioned on the phase-dependent attention structure.

Random eviction is immune to this mechanism: its decisions are independent of importance score quality. This explains the flat severity gradient ($G \approx 1$) observed under random eviction at all sparsity levels.

\subsection{Entropy Redistribution: Consistent in DeepSeek, Partial in Qwen}
\label{sec:entropy-redistribution}

Beyond discrimination collapse, we examine whether head pruning also redistributes the attention entropy of surviving heads. We measure the mean attention entropy per head:
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{H}_i^{(h)} = -\sum_{j=1}^{T} a_{ij}^{(h)} \log a_{ij}^{(h)}
    \label{eq:attn-entropy}
\end{equation}
and compute $\Delta\mathcal{H}$ as the change from baseline (no pruning) to the pruned condition.

For DeepSeek under importance-based eviction, the entropy delta is consistently negative across all four sparsity levels ($\Delta\mathcal{H} \in [-0.040, -0.047]$; Table~\ref{tab:entropy-deltas}), with the effect stable across reasoning phases. % exp_id: c4_entropy_deepseek
This is consistent with importance-based eviction systematically selecting heads whose removal concentrates attention in the surviving heads, reinforcing the discrimination collapse documented above.

For Qwen, the entropy pattern only partially replicates. The delta is negative at $s{=}0.15$ ($\Delta\mathcal{H} = -0.073$) and $s{=}0.20$ ($-0.076$), consistent with DeepSeek. However, at $s{=}0.25$, the delta reverses to $+0.045$, with 22 of 30 problems showing positive entropy change. At $s{=}0.30$, the delta is near zero ($-0.007$). % exp_id: c4_entropy_qwen

The $s{=}0.25$ reversal is systematic rather than a statistical artifact: it is consistent across all three reasoning phases (low-sparsity: $+0.042$, mid-sparsity: $+0.034$, high-sparsity: $+0.017$). The mid-sparsity phase contains fewer segments (median~26), which reduces statistical power for that phase; however, the reversal is equally present in the low-sparsity phase with substantially more segments, so mid-phase power limitations are a confound but do not account for the full pattern. We interpret this as a genuine model-specific difference, possibly arising from distinct head specialization patterns: Qwen's instruction tuning (vs.\ DeepSeek's distillation-based training) may produce head configurations where removing 25\% of heads triggers compensatory broadening rather than concentration.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Attention entropy deltas ($\Delta\mathcal{H}$) under importance-based eviction. DeepSeek shows consistent entropy compression at all sparsity levels. Qwen partially replicates: negative at $s{\leq}0.20$, reversed at $s{=}0.25$, near-zero at $s{=}0.30$.}
\label{tab:entropy-deltas}
\small
\begin{tabular}{llr}
\toprule
Model & $s$ & $\Delta\mathcal{H}$ \\
\midrule
DeepSeek & $0.15$ & $-0.040$ \\
DeepSeek & $0.20$ & $-0.047$ \\
DeepSeek & $0.25$ & $-0.045$ \\
DeepSeek & $0.30$ & $-0.044$ \\
\midrule
Qwen & $0.15$ & $-0.073$ \\
Qwen & $0.20$ & $-0.076$ \\
Qwen & $0.25$ & $+0.045$ \\
Qwen & $0.30$ & $-0.007$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The partial replication of entropy redistribution, combined with the full replication of discrimination collapse, suggests a two-level mechanistic picture: head pruning universally degrades the discriminative power of importance scores (as measured by Gini and CV), while the specific direction of entropy redistribution depends on model-specific head specialization patterns. Discrimination collapse is sufficient on its own to explain the emergent phase-strategy interaction; entropy redistribution, where it co-occurs, may amplify the effect.

\subsection{Implications for Compound Sparsity}
\label{sec:analysis-implications}

The discrimination collapse mechanism suggests several broader conclusions.

\paragraph{Architecture generality.} Discrimination collapse depends on two properties: (i)~attention-mass-based importance scoring for KV eviction, and (ii)~a sufficient number of pruned heads to degrade the importance distribution. Both are shared by the major KV cache compression methods---H$_2$O \cite{h2o2024}, SnapKV \cite{snapkv-neurips2024}, PyramidKV \cite{pyramidkv2024}---and by any autoregressive transformer producing extended reasoning traces. The cross-model consistency of the Gini and CV evidence (Table~\ref{tab:discrimination-metrics}) supports this generality. The entropy redistribution component, while consistent in DeepSeek, appears model-dependent; the full mechanism may thus manifest differently across architectures.

\paragraph{Testable prediction.} Any importance metric derived from attention mass should exhibit discrimination collapse under head pruning, with the severity gradient scaling with the pruning-induced Gini decrease. Metrics that derive importance from sources independent of attention---such as activation magnitude or gradient-based saliency---would not be subject to the same collapse and should produce flatter severity gradients.

\paragraph{Reframing compound severity.} The flat random baseline ($G \approx 1$) demonstrates that phase-modulated severity amplification is not an inherent cost of compound sparsity \emph{per se}. Rather, it is an emergent phase-strategy interaction---the consequence of applying a discrimination-sensitive eviction strategy in a regime where head pruning systematically degrades that discrimination. A phase-aware sparsity controller could, in principle, mitigate this interaction by adjusting the eviction budget or strategy per phase.

\section{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:experimental-setup}

\subsection{Models}
\label{sec:models}

We conduct experiments on two 7B-parameter instruction-tuned models that share the Qwen2 backbone architecture (28 Transformer layers, 28 attention heads with 4-group GQA):

\paragraph{DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B} serves as our primary evaluation target. This model was distilled from DeepSeek-R1 \cite{deepseek-r1} and produces explicit chain-of-thought traces delimited by \texttt{<think>}$\cdots$\texttt{</think>} tags, yielding sequences with pronounced phase structure---extended reasoning followed by a concise solution. The structured generation pattern makes it well-suited for studying how sparsity interacts with distinct reasoning phases.

\paragraph{Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct} \cite{qwen2.5} provides a cross-model replication. Although it shares the same backbone family, its instruction tuning does not enforce explicit reasoning delimiters, producing a different distribution of attention sparsity across the generation trajectory. Replication on this model tests whether our findings generalize beyond distillation-induced phase structure.

Both models are loaded in \texttt{bfloat16} precision with eager attention (i.e., without FlashAttention) to permit direct manipulation of attention weight matrices during sparsity intervention.\footnote{An earlier \texttt{float16} configuration produced sporadic NaN values in Layer~27 of DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B, affecting ${\sim}$8\% of logit positions. Switching to \texttt{bfloat16} eliminated all NaN occurrences; we additionally apply NaN-guarded filtering as a safety net.}


\subsection{Dataset}
\label{sec:dataset}

We evaluate on a 50-problem stratified subset of the MATH-500 benchmark \cite{math500}, sampling uniformly across problem categories (algebra, geometry, number theory, combinatorics, etc.) with a fixed seed for reproducibility. Mathematical reasoning tasks are a natural testbed for our study: they elicit long chain-of-thought sequences (up to 2048 tokens) in which the model alternates between exploratory reasoning and structured computation---phases that differ markedly in attention sparsity. Each problem is decoded greedily (\texttt{max\_new\_tokens}$=$2048) to eliminate sampling variance.


\subsection{Compound Sparsity Configuration}
\label{sec:sparsity-config}

We apply \emph{symmetric} compound sparsity: for a given sparsity level $s$, we prune the same fraction $s$ of attention heads and evict the same fraction $s$ of KV cache entries. We evaluate four sparsity levels $s \in \{0.15, 0.20, 0.25, 0.30\}$, spanning from mild to aggressive compression.

\paragraph{Sparsity conditions.} For each problem and each sparsity level, we compute perplexity under four conditions: (i)~\emph{dense}---no intervention; (ii)~\emph{attn-only}---head pruning without KV eviction; (iii)~\emph{kv-only}---KV eviction without head pruning; and (iv)~\emph{both}---simultaneous head pruning and KV eviction.

\paragraph{Eviction strategies.} We compare two KV cache eviction strategies:

\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{Importance-based.} Each KV position receives a score equal to its cumulative attention weight summed over all query positions and heads. To correct for the inherent causal bias---earlier positions accumulate more attention simply because they are attended to by more queries---we apply position normalization:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:importance}
    \alpha_j = \frac{\sum_{i,h} a_{i,h,j}}{\texttt{seq\_len} - j}, \quad j \in \{1, \dots, T\},
\end{equation}
where $a_{i,h,j}$ is the attention weight from query~$i$, head~$h$ to key~$j$, and the denominator counts the number of queries that can attend to position~$j$ under causal masking. Position~0 (BOS) is always retained. The $T \cdot s$ positions with the lowest normalized scores are evicted.

During masked forward passes, we guard against numerical instability from the interaction of head masking and KV eviction: zeroed-out heads that would otherwise produce $\infty \times 0 = \text{NaN}$ are handled via selective replacement (\texttt{torch.where}) prior to the softmax, and any layer producing NaN outputs is skipped from the sparsity loss computation.

\item \textbf{Random.} KV positions (excluding BOS) are evicted uniformly at random, providing a strategy-agnostic baseline. Head pruning under the random condition selects heads via \texttt{torch.randperm} rather than by importance.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Head pruning.} Under importance-based eviction, heads are ranked by their Frobenius norm across the full attention matrix $\mathbf{A}_\ell \in \mathbb{R}^{H \times T \times T}$ per layer $\ell$, and the bottom-$s$ fraction is masked. Under random eviction, heads are selected uniformly at random.


\subsection{Phase Segmentation}
\label{sec:phase-seg}

We segment each generated sequence into three \emph{reasoning phases} based on the local attention sparsity of each token position. For each query position $i$, we compute per-head sparsity as the fraction of attention weights falling below $0.01 \times \max_j a_{i,h,j}$, then average across heads to obtain a scalar sparsity value per position. The resulting sparsity trajectory is partitioned into terciles via quantile boundaries: \emph{low-sparsity} (bottom third, corresponding to diffuse attention during exploratory reasoning), \emph{mid-sparsity}, and \emph{high-sparsity} (top third, corresponding to focused attention during structured computation). Contiguous segments shorter than $\texttt{min\_phase\_length}=10$ tokens are merged into adjacent phases to suppress noise.


\subsection{Metrics}
\label{sec:metrics}

\paragraph{Perplexity.} For each sparsity condition $c \in \{d, a, k, b\}$ and each phase, we compute segment-level perplexity as
$\text{PPL}_c = \exp\!\bigl(\tfrac{1}{|I|}\sum_{i \in I} \mathcal{L}_c(x_i)\bigr)$,
where $I$ is the set of token indices belonging to the phase and $\mathcal{L}_c(x_i)$ is the per-token cross-entropy loss under condition $c$.

\paragraph{Interaction Index.} The interaction index (Equation~\eqref{eq:ii}) quantifies the synergistic (super-additive) effect of compound sparsity beyond the sum of individual interventions.
When $\mathrm{II} > 0$, the two sparsity mechanisms amplify each other; $\mathrm{II} = 0$ indicates additive (independent) degradation.

\paragraph{Severity Gradient.} To measure how the interaction varies across reasoning phases, we use the severity gradient (Equation~\eqref{eq:gradient}):
where $\widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\,\textit{low}}$ and $\widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\,\textit{high}}$ are the median interaction indices over the low-sparsity and high-sparsity phases, respectively. We report medians rather than means because the II distribution is heavily right-skewed (mean/median ratios up to 43:1 at $s{=}0.30$), making the median a more robust central tendency estimator.


\subsection{Statistical Analysis}
\label{sec:stats}

All confidence intervals and significance tests are computed via the nonparametric bootstrap with $B = 10{,}000$ resamples \cite{efron1993bootstrap}.

\paragraph{Per-problem bootstrap.} For each sparsity level and strategy, we resample the 50 per-problem II values with replacement and compute the mean of each resample, yielding a 95\% percentile confidence interval for the population mean II.

\paragraph{Paired bootstrap.} To test whether the severity gradient differs between importance-based and random eviction, we pair observations by problem (same problem, same sparsity level) and bootstrap the difference in gradients. This controls for problem-level variance in difficulty and sequence length.

\paragraph{Unpaired bootstrap.} We additionally compute per-strategy gradient confidence intervals without pairing, to verify that the importance-based gradient is significantly greater than 1 (i.e., phase modulation exists) independently of the random baseline.

All tests yield $p_{\text{Bonf}} < 0.005$ at every sparsity level, confirming that the observed phase--strategy interactions are not artifacts of sampling variability.\footnote{Experiments were conducted on a server with 4$\times$ NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell Server Edition 96\,GB GPUs, with one model--sparsity configuration per GPU.}

